## Narrative
The finding is real and reachable in the main runtime path. HEAD /{bucket} is routed to HeadBucket, unauthenticated requests can become anonymous, and authorize_head_bucket evaluates a ListBucket policy decision through a contextless helper. That helper only marks request headers absent; for_bucket leaves prefix, delimiter, and max_keys unavailable. The condition-key resolver treats unavailable values as InputUnavailable, and PolicyStatement::request_effect maps InputUnavailable to no effect even for explicit Deny. The resulting NoMatch then falls back to requester_can_read_bucket, which can allow public-read buckets. The validated PoC demonstrated contextless_decision=NoMatch while the same request with absent list parameters produced ExplicitDeny. Severity remains medium because exploitation is network-reachable and can be unauthenticated under plausible public bucket settings, but impact is narrow: HeadBucket metadata/existence disclosure and policy-intent bypass, not object data read/write/delete or full ListObjects access.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication
- anonymous AuthContext for missing auth
- bucket policy explicit Deny/Allow evaluation
- bucket ACL public-read fallback
- Public Access Block IgnorePublicAcls can disable public ACL fallback
- optional TLS via ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH/ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH
- connection and in-flight request semaphores
## Blindspots
- Static review only; no live deployment, ingress, reverse proxy, or actual public exposure artifacts were present in the repository.
- The validated PoC was an executable Rust/library-level reproduction rather than a full end-to-end HTTP deployment test, though HTTP reachability is supported by routing and dispatch code.
- Repository does not contain real bucket policies or ACL state, so the exact prevalence of the required public-read plus explicit Deny configuration is unknown.
- External controls such as network firewalls, reverse proxies, or operator-managed Public Access Block settings cannot be verified from the checkout.
